WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.740
TABBI helps people organize browser tabs,

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:04.950
and return to the materials they need.

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:07.590
As we switch between multiple tasks,

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:09.530
browser tabs keep accumulating.

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:11.670
We leave them open to read later,

00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:14.930
remember unfinished work, or avoid losing useful materials.

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:20.200
But without organization, we can forget why we opened a page, or what we intended to do with it.

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:23.040
The goal is not simply to close tabs.

00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:24.840
It is to group them by purpose,

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:27.730
while keeping the links and context that help us return.

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:29.820
TABBI connects two activities:

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:31.850
organizing and saving tabs,

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:33.730
then finding and reopening them.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:37.500
Saved task names, links, and notes connect the two.

00:00:37.955 --> 00:00:39.715
Here is the actual Chrome extension.

00:00:39.955 --> 00:00:41.695
It reads the tabs in the current window.

00:00:42.235 --> 00:00:45.205
The user describes their work and requests organization.

00:00:45.915 --> 00:00:50.515
TABBI suggests task groups based on tab titles, links, and saved tasks.

00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:52.550
The user reviews these suggestions,

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:55.530
renames a group, and moves tabs between tasks.

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:59.680
They can add materials to an existing task or keep them as a new task.

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:01.486
The proposal remains editable.

00:01:01.496 --> 00:01:03.936
A note can record what to do next.

00:01:04.536 --> 00:01:07.826
The user saves the selected tasks before closing their tabs.

00:01:08.286 --> 00:01:09.576
Other tabs remain open.

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:13.420
Later, the user asks for saved tabs about Generative UI.

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:18.030
TABBI recommends matching pages and asks whether to focus on papers or include

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:19.540
workshop materials and notes.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:23.410
The user can choose a suggested option or add a condition.

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:26.190
Here, the user asks to see only papers.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:27.860
TABBI narrows the results.

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:30.790
The user selects the relevant pages and opens them.

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:33.070
These are the actual saved pages,

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:35.390
rather than a simulated restored list.

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:37.240
The workflow starts with open tabs.

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:40.530
The model suggests groups, and the user reviews and saves them.

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:43.870
Task names, links, and notes preserve the context.

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:48.230
When the user returns, the agent uses that context to interpret their purpose,

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:51.490
asks a question if needed, and updates its recommendation.

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.640
The user chooses what to reopen.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:57.470
All three participants responded positively to automatic grouping.

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:01.370
They valued suggestions when they were unsure how to organize their materials,

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:04.580
and appreciated being able to review and edit the groups.

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:07.620
But agent assistance was not preferred in every situation.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:12.260
Participant one found that browsing the list helped rediscover forgotten materials.

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:15.620
Conversation required remembering what they wanted to find.

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:20.440
Participant two used a broad goal, planning a trip, and appreciated suggestions for

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:21.700
maps and AI tools.

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:25.430
Participant three preferred direct browsing with fewer than ten tabs,

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:28.020
and saw repeated questions as extra work.

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:31.230
Suggested buttons could still provide useful options.

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:34.360
The lesson is to offer agent assistance when it helps,

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:36.568
and direct access when it is simpler. Thank you.
